WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.120
Speaker 1: Let's start today with some very&nbsp;
interesting economic news, despite sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:11.120
being held up by Republicans as the example of&nbsp;
everything that is bad and that should not be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:18.320
done, and that is a disaster of the progressive&nbsp;
left. California's economy is leading the nation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:25.520
in growth and in fact, is growing almost as&nbsp;
quickly as the major developing nation of China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.000
if you can believe it. And you know, this&nbsp;
gloom and doom about lockdown's and liberal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.360
policies and all of this stuff, it really seems&nbsp;
not to have held back the state of California,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:42.160
which is number one in terms of GDP growth in&nbsp;
the United States, increasing household income,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:48.400
investing in innovation, a whole bunch of other&nbsp;
metrics. It's California that's number one. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:53.440
once again, and this has been I mean, go back&nbsp;
all the way to the beginning of Reaganomics and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.440 --> 00:01:00.480
supply side and trickle down economic realities&nbsp;
seem to increasingly be the exact opposite of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:07.680
what right wingers want you to believe in terms of&nbsp;
GDP growth. California has grown 22 percent since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:14.240
2015. These are incredible numbers. California has&nbsp;
grown on a percentage basis, more than Germany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:20.560
more than Japan, more than the United States&nbsp;
overall, and almost as much as China. China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:26.400
a developing nation that is experiencing a&nbsp;
rapid economic growth, expanding middle class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.840
etc.. China has grown twenty nine percent since&nbsp;
2015. California has grown almost to that amount.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.760
And in the midst of this, they're trying to&nbsp;
recall Democratic Governor Gavin Newsom. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:45.200
yet the state is growing almost as quickly as&nbsp;
China. California added one point three million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:49.760
people to its non-farm payrolls since&nbsp;
last April. Household income increased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:55.280
one hundred and sixty four billion dollars,&nbsp;
almost as much as Texas, Florida and Pennsylvania&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:01.760
combined. California's budget surplus. Yeah,&nbsp;
it's a surplus. You wouldn't know this from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:08.640
listening to Fox News about California. The&nbsp;
California surplus has grown to a record.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:16.000
Seventy five billion dollars revenue per employee&nbsp;
has grown in California much more quickly than in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:22.080
the rest of the United States. So there are some&nbsp;
really important takeaways here as we think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:29.120
what this sensible economic policy look like&nbsp;
at the state level. First of all, the constant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:34.000
endless ranting from Republicans about how&nbsp;
terrible everything is in California economically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:39.520
socially, culturally and otherwise. It's just&nbsp;
not true. Is California expensive? Absolutely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:47.200
California is expensive in the net. Our taxes&nbsp;
higher in California for someone of average income&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:52.080
than in many other states. That really depends.&nbsp;
There's this study that looked at Texas versus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.800
California for someone making I think it was&nbsp;
like between 60 and 80 thousand dollars a year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:03.840
And if you really account for all of the expenses&nbsp;
that are levied by the state in California versus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:09.440
Texas. And so you look at local and property sales&nbsp;
taxes, fees on cars and other different things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:16.080
it ends up being slightly cheaper to live in&nbsp;
California than Texas for the average person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:21.920
even accounting for the California state income&nbsp;
tax. But the more important takeaway is, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:27.360
more recently, the covid lockdown's, as they were&nbsp;
called, which not that many people in California&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.840
were even really abiding by the right, was saying&nbsp;
covid lockdown's are destroying California.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:38.400
They didn't destroy the state. California is doing&nbsp;
really well. California is in the top states in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:43.840
terms of portion of its population vaccinated,&nbsp;
which is sort of a logistical and infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.960
signal about California. So the bottom line is&nbsp;
California is expensive, but it's expensive for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:53.760
a reason. And you would think that Republicans&nbsp;
would understand this. The people who talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:58.880
the free market, the reason that California is&nbsp;
expensive is there's really high demand and a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.920
of people want to live there. Why do things cost&nbsp;
a lot? Sometimes things cost a lot because lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:10.560
people want to buy them. That's called the market.&nbsp;
Now, that being said, California, like every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:18.080
state, still has many problems that many metro&nbsp;
areas have because it's in demand. It prices out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:23.440
lots of people, including people who want to live&nbsp;
there but can't afford to. That's a real issue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:29.520
We can recognize. It's expensive because people&nbsp;
want to live there and that is a market based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:33.840
thing. But at the same time, a lot of people&nbsp;
are getting priced out. That's a real issue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.400
We still have huge inequality in both wealth&nbsp;
and income. California is no exception to that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.800
But the lies that you're being fed by people like&nbsp;
Tucker Carlson and Laura Ingraham and various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:49.040
of the Trumps and others about the dystopian&nbsp;
California, it's not true. The undeniable reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:56.080
is that California is the envy of the world in&nbsp;
terms of an economy. If California were a country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:02.240
it would be a significant country. Globally, and&nbsp;
it's just one of 50 states in the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:07.600
in terms of average standard of living, in&nbsp;
terms of innovation, in terms of technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:13.840
some of the best places in the world to live are&nbsp;
in California. There aren't in West Virginia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:18.080
Right. I'm not attacking West Virginia&nbsp;
or the people of West Virginia. But what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:23.360
I'm saying is there's a massive infrastructure&nbsp;
difference between California and West Virginia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:28.560
There is a massive education quality difference.&nbsp;
There's a massive health care quality difference.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.600
Wages are significantly higher. And yeah. So all&nbsp;
of that together makes California expensive and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:39.040
it makes West Virginia really cheap. It has to&nbsp;
do with politics. It has to do with demand. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:46.240
it's actually stunning that Republicans get away&nbsp;
with trashing California when it's an obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:52.160
incredibly dynamic economy that in many ways&nbsp;
is the envy of the world. California would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:57.920
be a globally successful country, and it's just&nbsp;
one of 50 states. And in the midst of this, are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:02.960
they going to recall the governor, Gavin Newsom? I&nbsp;
don't know. We will see and we're going to follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:09.680
it. But the doom and gloom about California, it&nbsp;
is just not backed up by the numbers. And they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:15.520
don't you know, it's it's obviously a transparent&nbsp;
political attack. There's no question about that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:23.520
But the degree to which it's transparently bogus&nbsp;
is only becoming more clear now as we start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:29.440
get this data as we emerge from the pandemic.&nbsp;
And they're not going to change their tune. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:35.120
hopefully you now will know that the bogus attacks&nbsp;
on California are just that. They're simply bogus

